The filings give the LIFO charge for 2022 through 2025, and the stated driver each time is product cost inflation.

- **2022:** The charge was $626 million. [1]
- **2023:** It fell to $113 million, which the filing attributes to lower product cost inflation than in 2022. [1]
- **2024:** The charge was $95 million, down from $113 million, again because product cost inflation was lower than in 2023. [2]
- **2025:** The charge rose to $157 million from $95 million, because product cost inflation was higher than in 2024. [3]

On the accounting side, about 92% of inventories in 2024 were valued using the LIFO method [4]. Kroger uses the Link-Chain, Dollar-Value LIFO method to calculate the charge. [4]